(D5 — Discrepancy 5 repro: on 0.4.9 and earlier, a DECLARED `float` or
 `map` return type was lost when the call result was printed directly
 rather than routed through a declared variable. The float printed as
 its IEEE-754 bit pattern (4612811918334230528 = 0x4004000000000000 =
 2.5) and the map as a raw heap address; the same two values printed
 correctly one line earlier, out of variables of the declared type.
 text, boolean and list were unaffected (FUN-40). This was the shape of
 vox bug #45, except that #45's cure — declaring the return type — was
 already applied here and the result was still wrong. The map address
 varies per run (ASLR), so this probe is compared on its EXIT CODE only:
 it exits 3 unconditionally after printing.
 RESOLVED, 2026-08-22 — vox #67 ("Declaring the return type is the first
 way out bug #45's diagnostic offers, so it now works for all eleven
 types"). Re-run verbatim against 0.4.10: `2.5` / `{"ann": 30}` (routed)
 then `2.5` / `{"ann": 30}` again (printed directly) — the two blocks
 now agree, where 0.4.9 printed the bit pattern and a raw address for
 the second block.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D5.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   exit 3
)
To 'give float'. Return a float, 2.5.
To 'give map'. Return a map, {"ann": 30}.

a float called 'routed float' is 'give float'.
a map called 'routed map' is 'give map'.
Print 'routed float'.
Print 'routed map'.
Print "--- the same two calls, printed directly ---".
Print 'give float'.
Print 'give map'.
Exit 3.
